From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Tue, 08 Sep 2026 00:23:38 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vhost: do not verify vring host mappings under Xen, it
 abort()s the device model

Enabling Vulkan (Venus) in DomA killed DomA's device model the instant Mesa
created its first Venus context. Measured on RPi4 hardware 2026-09-07, three
times on three freshly created domains, 100 % reproducible from
`adb shell cmd gpu vkjson`:

    info: xen-arm: device RAM 'blob' at gpa 0xc0000000, 33 pages, hva 0xffff6998e000
    info: xen-arm: shared 33 pages of device RAM into dom2 at 0xc0000000 (pagemap, ...)
    descriptor table relocated for ring 0
    Verify ring failure on region 0

then SIGABRT. From the coredump, the call chain is

    virgl_cmd_resource_map_blob            hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c:868
      virtio_gpu_virgl_map_resource_blob   hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c:188
        memory_region_transaction_commit   system/memory.c:1169
          vhost_commit                     hw/virtio/vhost.c:698  -> abort()

i.e. adding the blob's MemoryRegion to the guest's address space drives the
vhost MemoryListener, and vhost_commit() re-verifies every vring against the
rebuilt memory table.

That verification cannot succeed under Xen. qemu does not map the whole of a
Xen guest's RAM -- from the same coredump:

    sect[0] mr name=xen.ram  gpa=0x40000000 size=0x80000000
            rb->idstr=xen.ram  rb->host=(nil)          <-- no host mapping
    region[0] guest_phys_addr=0x40000000 userspace_addr=0x40000000   <-- hva == gpa

memory_region_get_ram_ptr() returns NULL for that section. vhost_commit()
computes userspace_addr as that pointer plus section->offset_within_region, so
with the base NULL what it records is the offset alone -- and for xen.ram the
offset within the region happens to be the guest physical address, which is why
the value reads as a pseudo-hva equal to the gpa. Meanwhile vq->desc holds a
real map-cache pointer:

    reg_gpa  = 0x40000000   reg_hva  = 0x40000000
    ring_gpa = 0xb0106000   ring_hva = 0xffffb83b6000
    expected = reg_hva + (ring_gpa - reg_gpa) = 0xb0106000  !=  0xffffb83b6000  -> -EBUSY

So the "check that ring's MemoryRegion wasn't replaced" test reports every ring
as relocated. The ring had not moved -- 0xffffb83b6000 was still live; only the
recorded region hva was never an hva at all. All three of DomA's vhost devices
(two vhost-net, one vhost-vsock) carry region[0] userspace_addr = 0x40000000,
which is why turning vhost off on the netdevs only moved the abort onto the
vsock device (region 0 -> region 2, measured).

This is therefore not blob- or Venus-specific: ANY memory topology change that
occurs while a vhost device has ->started set will abort the device model on a
Xen guest. It has simply never been reached before, because vhost_dev_start()
commits before it sets ->started, and a virtio-gpu host-visible blob mapping is
the first post-start topology change this configuration has ever produced.

Address translation for these backends is done by domid, not by host VA, so the
mapping identity this function tries to police is not the one in use. Skip the
check when Xen is in use. vhost_commit() still rebuilds and pushes the memory
table itself; only the (meaningless, fatal) verification is dropped.

vhost_dev_has_iommu() already returns early here for the same reason -- when
translation is somebody else's job, there is nothing to compare -- so this sits
next to it.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending [not yet submitted to qemu-devel; the check it skips
 cannot succeed under Xen, where qemu never maps the whole of a guest's RAM.
 Note that the vhost backends this unblocks get their Xen address translation
 from the fork's own guest-side vhost-Xen work, which upstream does not have]
---
--- a/hw/virtio/vhost.c
+++ b/hw/virtio/vhost.c
@@ -27,6 +27,7 @@
 #include "migration/blocker.h"
 #include "migration/qemu-file-types.h"
 #include "system/dma.h"
+#include "system/xen.h"
 #include "trace.h"
 
 /* enabled until disconnected backend stabilizes */
@@ -520,6 +521,33 @@
         return 0;
     }
 
+    /*
+     * Under Xen there is nothing here to verify, and verifying anyway is fatal.
+     *
+     * qemu does not map the whole of a Xen guest's RAM: the RAMBlock backing
+     * "xen.ram" has host == NULL and the vrings are reached on demand through
+     * the map cache (xen_map_cache()). memory_region_get_ram_ptr() therefore
+     * returns NULL for those sections, so the userspace_addr that
+     * vhost_commit() records in the vhost memory table is a pseudo-hva equal to
+     * the guest physical address -- while vq->desc holds a real map-cache
+     * pointer. The "ring's MemoryRegion wasn't replaced" comparison below can
+     * then never hold, so every ring looks "relocated" and vhost_commit()
+     * abort()s the device model on the FIRST memory topology change that
+     * happens after the device was started. vhost_dev_start() commits before it
+     * sets ->started, which is the only reason this is not hit at boot.
+     *
+     * On Arm that first post-start change is a virtio-gpu host-visible blob
+     * being mapped into the guest (Venus/Vulkan), so enabling Vulkan in a guest
+     * killed its device model as soon as Mesa created its first Venus context.
+     * Address translation for these backends is done by domid rather than by
+     * host VA, so the mapping identity this function tries to police is not the
+     * one actually in use. Skip it; vhost_commit() still updates the memory
+     * table itself below.
+     */
+    if (xen_enabled()) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+
     for (i = 0; i < dev->nvqs; ++i) {
         struct vhost_virtqueue *vq = dev->vqs + i;
 
